--
template scope 'rebind' is ambiguous
--
KNOWNBUG: N5008 [basic.lookup.qual]/[temp.names]/3 -- `T::template
rebind<int>` lookup fails ("template scope 'rebind' is ambiguous",
listed in the forbidden-patterns section above) because the completed
__alloc_traits<> instance's scope lacks its never-used member template
and the fallback collects every same-name member template program-wide.
cvise-reduced (39 lines, header-free) from
cpp17_hash_node_vector_alloc; the alias chain mirrors libstdc++'s
__uset_hashtable and the hash<vector> partial-spec declaration -- each
part is needed for the instantiation order.  Front-end recovery
currently keeps verification green, so the FORBIDDEN pattern is what
makes this fail today.  Flip to CORE when the lookup is fixed.

DIAGNOSIS SHARPENED 2026-07-17 (fix attempt): the primary-template-
scope fallback for `T::template rebind<args>` ([temp.names]/3) is NOT
sufficient because the primary template's class-BODY scope does not
exist either -- scope-tree BFS from the primary's template scope finds
only the instance scope (empty) and the template-argument sub-scope.
Member class templates only obtain scope entries when they are USED
during some instance's own instantiation.  The real fix is to REGISTER
member class-template declarations in the instance scope when a class
template instance's body is converted (instantiate_template /
typecheck_compound_body), mirroring what the template_methods walk
already does for member FUNCTION templates ("register in the
instantiated class scope so overload resolution can find it").

MINIMALITY 2026-07-17: a fourth targeted hand-mimic (competitor member
template registered first, victim instance completed through an alias
chain with a value-dependent default argument, consumer reaching
rebind through a dependent scope) still PASSES -- the 39-line cvise
reduction is genuinely minimal; the incomplete `struct K`, the
`hash<vector<_Alloc>>` partial-specialization declaration and the
allocator_traits::value_type member interlock to produce the failing
instantiation order and cannot be simplified away by hand.
